<title>LATVIA: SWEDBANK SEES 3 TO 4 PCT GROWTH IN BALTICS IN 1997/98.</title>
<headline>SWEDBANK SEES 3 TO 4 PCT GROWTH IN BALTICS IN 1997/98.</headline>
<dateline>RIGA 1997-04-22</dateline>
<text>
<p>Sweden's Swedbank said on Tuesday</p>
<p>that it sees continued growth in the Baltic economies, with some</p>
<p>three to four percent growth expected in 1997/98.</p>
<p>The bank said in an analysis of eastern Europe, including</p>
<p>Latvia, Lithuania and Estonia, that there were good export</p>
<p>opportunities for Swedish firms in the Baltic region.</p>
<p>Average GDP growth in the 10 countries around the Baltic Sea</p>
<p>was expected this year to be 2.8 percent against 2.6 percent in</p>
<p>1996, bigger than the EU average.</p>
<p>Swedbank gave the following figures for the three Baltic</p>
<p>countries. All the figures are in percent.</p>
<p>		 GDP		CPI	 CURRENT	 BUDGET</p>
<p>		 GROWTH			 ACCOUNT	 BALANCE</p>
<p>						 (pct of GDP)  (pct of GDP)</p>
<p>		96  97  98   96  97  98   96   97  98    96  97  98</p>
<p> ESTONIA   4.0 4.0 4.0   15  12  10  -6.0 -6.0 --   -1.0 -0.5 0</p>
<p> LATVIA    2.5 3.0 3.5   14  12  12  -3.5 -3.0 --   -2.0 -1.0 0</p>
<p> LITHUANIA 3.5 3.5 4.0   14  15  15  -6.0 --   --   -1.7 -1.5 --</p>
<p>-- Martinsh Gravitis, Riga Newsroom, +371 721 5240</p>
</text>
